IMPORT prelude

-- Public Trustee Act 1941 (Western Australia) -- when the Public Trustee may take over the estate of someone who has
-- died: by an order to administer (s 10, s 12), by an election without any grant where the estate is small (s 14), or as
-- the executor a will names (ss 7, 8); what it may charge for doing so (ss 9, 38A, 38B, 47A, 62); what it may do with a
-- child's small share (s 18); and when an unclaimed balance goes to the State (s 45).
--
-- SEAT: the next of kin of a person who has died leaving a small estate in Western Australia -- the child, spouse,
-- parent or other relative who would otherwise take out the grant -- and, for ss 7-8, the person making a will who wants
-- the Public Trustee as executor. Every decision is phrased as that person would ask it: "May the Public Trustee
-- administer without a grant", "May we hand the estate to the Public Trustee", "What is the most the Public Trustee may
-- charge".
--
-- Provisions covered: s 2 (client, administration), s 7(1), s 8, s 9 (no charge for the vesting), s 10(1)(a)-(g), the
-- proviso to s 10(1)(ab), s 10(4)-(5), s 11, s 12(1)-(3), s 14(1)-(2), (4), (5), (6), s 18, s 38A(2), s 38B(1)-(6), (8),
-- (9), s 45(1)-(2), s 47A(1)-(3), s 62; Public Trustee Regulations 1942 reg 10A; Trustee Companies Regulations 1988 reg 4.
--
-- Edition: the compilation incorporating amendments up to the Statutes (Repeals and Minor Amendments) Act 2025 (No. 14 of
-- 2025, s 33, in operation 26 September 2025, deleting s 15); the Liquor Control Amendment Act 2025 Pt 5 Div 3 is listed
-- as uncommenced and does not touch the sections encoded; as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49053.txt (legislation.wa.gov.au law_a649, retrieved 7 September
-- 2026). Regulations used: the Public Trustee Regulations 1942, version 04-b0-08 in force from 1 July 2008, as deposited
-- at ../../../public-trustee-regulations-1942/registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_7511.txt (retrieved 20 October 2026): reg
-- 10A, "The prescribed amount referred to in section 45(2) of the Act is $1 500", and Forms 3, 3A, 3B and 4 (the s 14
-- election and its revocation). Those Regulations do NOT prescribe the s 14 amount: s 14(6) defines it as "the amount
-- prescribed for the purposes of section 10 of the Trustee Companies Act 1987". That amount is set by the Trustee
-- Companies Regulations 1988 reg 4, "For the purposes of section 10 of the Act the amount prescribed is $50 000", version
-- 01-b0-06 in force from 5 January 2013, as deposited at
-- ../../../trustee-companies-regulations-1988/registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_24509.txt (retrieved 21 October 2026); reg 4
-- carries no amendment note, so $50 000 has stood since the Regulations commenced on 1 September 1988. The Public
-- Trustee's scale of fees (s 38A, published in the Gazette) is not deposited; the scale fee is an INPUT.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: s 10(2)-(3) and (6) (the Court's conditions, the effect of an order, the Gazette notice), s 12(4)-(8)
-- (transfer after a grant, several executors, consents), s 12A (the Public Trustee as agent), s 13 (removal of an
-- executor), s 14(3) (the Gazette notice), s 16 (interstate balances), s 17 (escheat), Division 3 onwards (trusts,
-- represented persons, custodian trustee, investment and the Common Account, except s 38B(8)), s 54 (deposit of wills),
-- s 55 (inquiries), ss 56-61 and 63. Machinery out: ss 1-6B, 37B-37C, 39-44A, 46-48, 64-65. The Administration Act 1903
-- (who takes, and the grant the family may take out itself) has its own baseline encoding, as does the Trustee Companies
-- Act 1987 route if one is written.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the
-- individual to whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Public Trustee Act 1941 -- the next of kin's questions about a small estate`

§§ `the facts the family can state`

-- s 10(1) and s 14(1) both begin with a person who "dies testate or intestate, in or out of Western Australia, leaving
-- property in Western Australia". "Gross value ... as estimated by the Public Trustee" is the Public Trustee's
-- estimate, but the family can state it once given; it is asked as a fact.
DECLARE `The estate` HAS
    `left property in Western Australia`                                     IS A BOOLEAN
    `left property outside Western Australia`                                IS A BOOLEAN
    `gross value in Western Australia as estimated by the Public Trustee`    IS A NUMBER
    `the deceased left a will`                                               IS A BOOLEAN
    `someone has taken out probate or administration in Western Australia`   IS A BOOLEAN
    `days since the death`                                                   IS A NUMBER
    `full months since the death`                                            IS A NUMBER

-- What the family has done or can do. "Willing", "capable" and "resident within the jurisdiction" are the words of s
-- 10(1)(a) and (ab). "The person first entitled to the administration" of an intestate estate is fixed by the
-- Administration Act 1903 s 25; it is asked here as a fact (a borrowed term).
DECLARE `The family` HAS
    `an executor named in the will is willing, capable and resident in WA`               IS A BOOLEAN
    `the person first entitled to administration is willing, capable and resident in WA` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the executor renounced and everyone first entitled declined in writing filed in Court` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the estate is unprotected or liable to waste and the family is absent or not found` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the estate is perishable or in danger of being lost or destroyed`                   IS A BOOLEAN
    `persons entitled to obtain administration`                                          IS A NUMBER
    `of them, persons who have authorised the Public Trustee to apply`                   IS A NUMBER
    `the will expressly prohibits the executor authorising the Public Trustee`           IS A BOOLEAN

-- The judgments s 10(1) leaves to the Court, answered conditionally (2026-10-18 default).
DECLARE `What the Court thinks` HAS
    `there is no reasonable possibility of an application within 3 months`               IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is expedient or proper for the Public Trustee to administer`                     IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `s 12(1)-(3) -- handing the estate to the Public Trustee ourselves`

-- s 12(1): "Any person or the majority of the persons entitled to obtain administration (with the will of any testator
-- annexed) ... may authorise the Public Trustee to apply to the Court for and obtain an order to administer the estate."
-- s 12(3): the same for "the persons entitled to obtain administration of the estate of any intestate".
-- s 12(2): "Any person or a majority of the persons named ... as executor in any will may, unless expressly prohibited by
-- the will, authorise the Public Trustee".
-- READING TAKEN (an inference): "any person or the majority" means the one person where there is one, and a majority
-- where there are several. On the other reading any one of several could authorise alone.
@export May we authorise the Public Trustee to apply to administer the estate (Public Trustee Act 1941 s 12(1)-(3))?
GIVEN `the estate` IS AN `The estate` @desc The deceased's estate
      `the family` IS A `The family` @desc Who is entitled and who has authorised the Public Trustee
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have we authorised the Public Trustee to apply` IF
        `the family`'s `persons entitled to obtain administration` AT LEAST 1
    AND (`the family`'s `of them, persons who have authorised the Public Trustee to apply` TIMES 2)
            GREATER THAN `the family`'s `persons entitled to obtain administration`
    AND (    (NOT `the estate`'s `the deceased left a will`)
          OR (NOT `the family`'s `the will expressly prohibits the executor authorising the Public Trustee`))

§§ `s 10(1), s 12 -- may the Public Trustee apply for an order to administer`

-- s 10(1): "the Court may, on the application of the Public Trustee, grant to the Public Trustee an order to administer
-- the estate of such deceased person in any of the following cases --
--   (a) where such person dies testate, but leaves no executor willing and capable of acting ... resident within the
--       jurisdiction;
--   (ab) where such person dies intestate, and the person first entitled to the administration of his estate is
--       unwilling to act or incapable of acting ... or is not resident within the jurisdiction ...;
--   (b) where the executor renounces probate ... and all the persons first entitled to administration by writing filed
--       in the office of the Court decline to apply for administration;
--   (c) where probate or administration is not applied for within 3 months after the death ...;
--   (d) where, after the expiration of 30 days from such death, there appears to the Court to be no reasonable
--       possibility of application being made within such period as aforesaid;
--   (e) where the estate ... is unprotected or liable to waste and the executor or [partner] or next of kin is absent
--       from the locality ... or is not known or has not been found;
--   (f) where the estate ... is of a perishable nature or is in danger of being lost or destroyed;
--   (g) in any other case where the Court considers it expedient or proper."
-- s 2: "administration ... include[s] an order to administer" -- an order to administer IS a grant.
DECLARE `Ground for the Public Trustee` IS ONE OF
    `s 10(1)(a), no willing, capable executor resident in WA`
    `s 10(1)(ab), the person first entitled is unwilling, incapable or not resident in WA`
    `s 10(1)(b), the executor renounced and those first entitled declined`
    `s 10(1)(c), no application within 3 months of the death`
    `s 10(1)(d), after 30 days the Court sees no reasonable possibility of an application`
    `s 10(1)(e), the estate is unprotected and the family absent or not found`
    `s 10(1)(f), the estate is perishable or in danger`
    `s 12, the persons entitled have authorised the Public Trustee`
    `s 10(1)(g), the Court considers it expedient or proper`
    `no ground: the Public Trustee may not apply`

@export On what ground, if any, may the Public Trustee apply for an order to administer the estate (Public Trustee Act 1941 s 10(1), s 12)?
GIVEN `the estate` IS AN `The estate` @desc The deceased's estate
      `the family` IS A `The family` @desc What the executor and next of kin are doing
      `the Court` IS A `What the Court thinks` @desc The Court's view on the matters s 10(1)(d) and (g) leave to it
GIVETH A `Ground for the Public Trustee`
DECIDE `On what ground may the Public Trustee apply` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the estate`'s `left property in Western Australia`
            THEN `no ground: the Public Trustee may not apply`
        IF `the estate`'s `the deceased left a will`
           AND (NOT `the family`'s `an executor named in the will is willing, capable and resident in WA`)
            THEN `s 10(1)(a), no willing, capable executor resident in WA`
        IF (NOT `the estate`'s `the deceased left a will`)
           AND (NOT `the family`'s `the person first entitled to administration is willing, capable and resident in WA`)
            THEN `s 10(1)(ab), the person first entitled is unwilling, incapable or not resident in WA`
        IF `the family`'s `the executor renounced and everyone first entitled declined in writing filed in Court`
            THEN `s 10(1)(b), the executor renounced and those first entitled declined`
        IF (NOT `the estate`'s `someone has taken out probate or administration in Western Australia`)
           AND `the estate`'s `full months since the death` AT LEAST 3
            THEN `s 10(1)(c), no application within 3 months of the death`
        IF `the estate`'s `days since the death` GREATER THAN 30
           AND `the Court`'s `there is no reasonable possibility of an application within 3 months`
            THEN `s 10(1)(d), after 30 days the Court sees no reasonable possibility of an application`
        IF `the family`'s `the estate is unprotected or liable to waste and the family is absent or not found`
            THEN `s 10(1)(e), the estate is unprotected and the family absent or not found`
        IF `the family`'s `the estate is perishable or in danger of being lost or destroyed`
            THEN `s 10(1)(f), the estate is perishable or in danger`
        IF `Have we authorised the Public Trustee to apply` `the estate` `the family`
            THEN `s 12, the persons entitled have authorised the Public Trustee`
        IF `the Court`'s `it is expedient or proper for the Public Trustee to administer`
            THEN `s 10(1)(g), the Court considers it expedient or proper`
        OTHERWISE `no ground: the Public Trustee may not apply`

@export May the Public Trustee apply for an order to administer the estate (Public Trustee Act 1941 s 10(1), s 12)?
GIVEN `the estate` IS AN `The estate` @desc The deceased's estate
      `the family` IS A `The family` @desc What the executor and next of kin are doing
      `the Court` IS A `What the Court thinks` @desc The Court's view on the matters s 10(1)(d) and (g) leave to it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the Public Trustee apply for an order to administer` IF
    NOT (`On what ground may the Public Trustee apply` `the estate` `the family` `the Court`
             EQUALS `no ground: the Public Trustee may not apply`)

-- s 10(1)(ab) proviso: "if some other person in the State who, if it were not for this paragraph, would be entitled to
-- such administration applies for such administration, then the Court may grant administration to such person".
@export If the Public Trustee relies on s 10(1)(ab), may the Court grant administration to me instead because I live in WA and have applied?
GIVEN `I live in Western Australia`                    IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are in the State
      `I would otherwise be entitled to administration` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you would be entitled to administration but for s 10(1)(ab)
      `I have applied for administration`              IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the Court grant administration to me instead` IF
    `I live in Western Australia` AND `I would otherwise be entitled to administration` AND `I have applied for administration`

-- s 11: "The Public Trustee shall be entitled to a grant of administration ... in preference to any creditor applying in
-- that capacity ..., unless the creditor proves to the satisfaction of the Court that it will be more beneficial to the
-- estate that it should be administered by such creditor."
@export Is the Public Trustee preferred to a creditor who applies to administer the estate (s 11)?
GIVEN `the creditor proves to the Court it would be more beneficial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied the creditor administering would benefit the estate more
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the Public Trustee preferred to a creditor` IF
    NOT `the creditor proves to the Court it would be more beneficial`

§§ `s 14 -- the small-estate election, without any grant`

-- s 14(1): where a person dies "leaving property in Western Australia the gross value of which as estimated by the Public
-- Trustee does not at the time of the election ... exceed the prescribed amount and no person has taken out probate or
-- administration in Western Australia, the Public Trustee may, in all cases where he is entitled to apply for an order to
-- administer, in lieu of obtaining such order, file in the office of the Court an election in writing ... electing to
-- administer."
-- s 14(2): on filing, the Public Trustee "shall be deemed to be administrator of the whole property of the deceased
-- person in all respects as if a grant of probate or letters of administration ... had been regularly granted to him."
-- s 14(6): "prescribed amount means the amount prescribed for the purposes of section 10 of the Trustee Companies Act
-- 1987 for the relevant time." NOT in the Public Trustee Regulations 1942 (their reg 10A is the s 45(2) amount). Trustee
-- Companies Regulations 1988 reg 4: "For the purposes of section 10 of the Act the amount prescribed is $50 000." Not
-- amended since 1 September 1988, so it is the amount for any election made from then until the deposited version is
-- replaced.
`the s 14 prescribed amount` MEANS 50000
@export May the Public Trustee administer the estate without any grant, by filing a small-estate election, the estate being worth no more than $50 000 (Public Trustee Act 1941 s 14(1); Trustee Companies Regulations 1988 reg 4)?
GIVEN `the estate` IS AN `The estate` @desc The deceased's estate
      `the family` IS A `The family` @desc What the executor and next of kin are doing
      `the Court` IS A `What the Court thinks` @desc The Court's view on the matters s 10(1)(d) and (g) leave to it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the Public Trustee administer without a grant` IF
        `the estate`'s `left property in Western Australia`
    AND `the estate`'s `gross value in Western Australia as estimated by the Public Trustee` AT MOST `the s 14 prescribed amount`
    AND (NOT `the estate`'s `someone has taken out probate or administration in Western Australia`)
    AND `May the Public Trustee apply for an order to administer` `the estate` `the family` `the Court`

-- s 14(4): "If after filing such election the gross value of the property to be administered is found to exceed the
-- prescribed amount, or the property to which the election relates is found to include property outside the State, the
-- Public Trustee shall, as soon as practicable thereafter, file ... a memorandum ... and proceed in the ordinary manner to
-- obtain an order to administer the estate." (Form 4 of the Regulations calls the memorandum a revocation.)
@export After a small-estate election, must the Public Trustee go to the Court for an order after all (s 14(4))?
GIVEN `the gross value found` IS A NUMBER @desc The gross value of the property to be administered, as found after the election, in dollars
      `property outside WA has been found` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the property turns out to include property outside Western Australia
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the Public Trustee seek an order after all` IF
    `the gross value found` GREATER THAN `the s 14 prescribed amount` OR `property outside WA has been found`

-- s 14(5): "If after filing such election the Public Trustee finds that the deceased person who was supposed to have died
-- intestate has died testate, the Public Trustee shall, as soon as practicable, file ... a memorandum ... revoking such
-- election; whereupon such election shall be deemed to have been revoked accordingly, and the Public Trustee shall file
-- ... his accounts of all transactions in the matter of the estate".
@export If a will turns up after the Public Trustee elected to administer an estate thought intestate, must the election be revoked and accounts filed (s 14(5))?
GIVEN `the election treated the deceased as intestate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the election was made on the footing that there was no will
      `a will has been found`                          IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Public Trustee has found that the deceased left a will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the election be revoked and accounts filed` IF
    `the election treated the deceased as intestate` AND `a will has been found`

-- s 10(4)-(5): where every grantee has died leaving part of the estate unadministered, "the gross value of the part ... so
-- left unadministered ... does not exceed the sum of $10 000, as estimated by the Public Trustee, and no person has ...
-- taken out letters of administration de bonis non", the Public Trustee may, where entitled to apply for an order, file an
-- election instead, and is then "deemed to be administrator of the original estate left unadministered".
`the de bonis non ceiling` MEANS 10000

@export Where everyone who held the grant has died and part of the estate is left unadministered, may the Public Trustee finish it by election, without a new grant (s 10(4))?
GIVEN `every grantee has died`                       IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether everyone to whom probate or administration was granted has died
      `gross value left unadministered`              IS A NUMBER  @desc The Public Trustee's estimate of the unadministered part, in dollars
      `a grant de bonis non has been taken out`      IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether anyone has taken out letters of administration de bonis non since
      `the Public Trustee is entitled to apply`      IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Public Trustee could apply for an order to administer
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the Public Trustee finish the estate by election` IF
        `every grantee has died`
    AND `gross value left unadministered` AT MOST `the de bonis non ceiling`
    AND (NOT `a grant de bonis non has been taken out`)
    AND `the Public Trustee is entitled to apply`

§§ `ss 7, 8 -- the Public Trustee as executor`

-- s 7(1): where "any person ... can appoint a trustee, executor, administrator ... any of such appointments may be made of
-- the Public Trustee." s 8: "Whenever the Public Trustee has been or shall be named as executor in the last will and
-- testament or in any codicil ..., it shall be lawful for the Public Trustee to act as executor, and to apply for and
-- obtain probate".
@export May the Public Trustee act as executor of the will and obtain probate (Public Trustee Act 1941 ss 7-8)?
GIVEN `the will or a codicil names the Public Trustee as executor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Public Trustee is named as executor in the will or a codicil
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the Public Trustee act as executor` IF
    `the will or a codicil names the Public Trustee as executor`

-- s 2: "client means -- (a) a beneficiary of the estate of a deceased person which is administered by the Public Trustee;
-- (b) the donor of a power of attorney ... under which the Public Trustee is the donee ...; (c) a person who appoints the
-- Public Trustee to be the executor of the person's will; (d) a person on whose behalf moneys are invested in a Fund;
-- (e) a beneficiary of a trust administered by the Public Trustee; (f) a person who has appointed the Public Trustee to
-- act as the person's agent; or (g) a member of a class of persons prescribed by the regulations". No class is
-- prescribed in the deposited Regulations (inference from reading them).
DECLARE `My dealing with the Public Trustee` IS ONE OF
    `a beneficiary of an estate the Public Trustee administers`
    `the donor of a power of attorney to the Public Trustee`
    `I have appointed the Public Trustee executor of my will`
    `money is invested for me in a Public Trustee Fund`
    `a beneficiary of a trust the Public Trustee administers`
    `I have appointed the Public Trustee my agent`
    `next of kin of an estate administered by someone else`

@export Am I a client of the Public Trustee (s 2)?
GIVEN `my dealing` IS A `My dealing with the Public Trustee` @desc How you deal with the Public Trustee
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I a client of the Public Trustee` IF
    NOT (`my dealing` EQUALS `next of kin of an estate administered by someone else`)

§§ `s 18 -- a child's small share`

-- s 18: "Where the net amount payable to any infant out of any testate or intestate estate of which probate or
-- administration has been granted to the Public Trustee is under $5 000, the Public Trustee may pay ... the distributive
-- share ... to a parent of the infant, or to any person having the care or custody of such infant".
`the infant share ceiling` MEANS 5000

@export May the Public Trustee pay my child's share of the estate to me, as parent or carer (s 18)?
GIVEN `the Public Trustee holds the grant`        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether probate or administration (including an order to administer or a s 14 election) is held by the Public Trustee
      `the net amount payable to the child`       IS A NUMBER  @desc The child's net share, in dollars
      `I am a parent or have care or custody`     IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the child's parent or have the child's care or custody
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the Public Trustee pay my child's share to me` IF
        `the Public Trustee holds the grant`
    AND `the net amount payable to the child` LESS THAN `the infant share ceiling`
    AND `I am a parent or have care or custody`

§§ `ss 9, 38A, 38B, 47A, 62 -- what the Public Trustee may charge`

-- s 9: pending a grant the estate "shall be deemed to vest in the Public Trustee, but without any charge being leviable
-- therefor". s 38B(8): "There shall not be any fee payable to the Public Trustee in respect of income earned by an estate
-- by way of interest payable ... under section 39A(7)." s 38B(6): "a reasonable fee or remuneration for work involved in
-- the preparation and lodging of returns ... (other than probate, death, succession or estate duties)". s 38B(3):
-- reimbursement "for all expenses properly incurred". s 47A(1)-(2): a fee for "the preparation of a will or an enduring
-- power of attorney" only if "prepared under the direction and control of a legal practitioner". s 38 "fees includes
-- commissions and other charges".
DECLARE `What the charge is for` IS ONE OF
    `the estate vesting in the Public Trustee before a grant`
    `interest the Common Account pays the estate`
    `administering the estate, under the published scale`
    `returns for taxes other than death duties`
    `expenses properly incurred`
    `a will prepared under a legal practitioner's direction and control`
    `a will prepared without a legal practitioner's direction and control`

@export May the Public Trustee charge the estate or me for this (ss 9, 38B, 47A)?
GIVEN `the charge` IS A `What the charge is for` @desc What the fee, commission or charge is for
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the Public Trustee charge for this` IF
    CONSIDER `the charge`
    WHEN `the estate vesting in the Public Trustee before a grant` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `interest the Common Account pays the estate` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a will prepared without a legal practitioner's direction and control` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 38A(2): "A scale of fees has no effect unless it is published in the Gazette." s 38B(1): fees "not exceeding those
-- ... set out in the latest published scale of fees before the Public Trustee commences to perform the function or
-- provide the service." s 38B(2): nothing prevents fees "that a testator in his will has directed to be paid" or "that
-- have been agreed on ..., either in addition to or instead of the fees provided for by this section." s 38B(9): the
-- Public Trustee "may waive, either wholly or in part, any fees" if "satisfied that there is proper cause" -- answered
-- conditionally, as the amount waived. Expenses (s 38B(3)) and tax-return work (s 38B(6)) come on top.
DECLARE `The fee facts` HAS
    `a scale was published in the Gazette before the work began`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `fee under the latest scale published before the work began`    IS A NUMBER
    `fee the will directs`                                          IS A NUMBER
    `fee agreed with the Public Trustee`                            IS A NUMBER
    `the directed or agreed fee is instead of the scale fee`        IS A BOOLEAN
    `expenses properly incurred`                                    IS A NUMBER
    `reasonable fee for tax returns other than death duties`        IS A NUMBER
    `scale fee waived by the Public Trustee`                        IS A NUMBER

@export What is the most the Public Trustee may take from the estate in fees and expenses (ss 38A, 38B)?
GIVEN `the fees` IS A `The fee facts` @desc The scale, any directed or agreed fee, expenses and any waiver
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most the Public Trustee may charge` IS
    LET `scale part` IS
            IF `the fees`'s `a scale was published in the Gazette before the work began`
            THEN `the fees`'s `fee under the latest scale published before the work began`
                     MINUS `the fees`'s `scale fee waived by the Public Trustee`
            ELSE 0
        `directed and agreed` IS `the fees`'s `fee the will directs` PLUS `the fees`'s `fee agreed with the Public Trustee`
        `fee part` IS
            IF `the fees`'s `the directed or agreed fee is instead of the scale fee`
            THEN `directed and agreed`
            ELSE `scale part` PLUS `directed and agreed`
    IN `fee part`
       PLUS `the fees`'s `expenses properly incurred`
       PLUS `the fees`'s `reasonable fee for tax returns other than death duties`

-- s 38B(4): fees "are payable out of the capital or income of the estate". s 62: "The fees and commissions charged by the
-- Public Trustee shall be deemed to be a testamentary expense." So they come out of the estate before the next of kin
-- take, not from the next of kin personally.
@export Are the Public Trustee's fees paid out of the estate, as a testamentary expense, rather than by me personally (ss 38B(4), 62)?
GIVEN `the fee is for administering the estate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the fee is charged for the administration or management of the estate
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Are the fees paid out of the estate` IF
    `the fee is for administering the estate`

§§ `s 45 -- an unclaimed balance and the State`

-- s 45(1): on or before 31 March each year the Public Trustee shall credit to the Consolidated Account sums held "to the
-- credit of any trust or estate ... for a term of 6 years next preceding and in respect whereof the Public Trustee has no
-- information or knowledge of the existence of any person entitled in distribution, or claiming so to be".
-- s 45(2): a sum "that is equal to or exceeds the prescribed amount shall not be paid ... unless the Public Trustee has
-- caused an advertisement in respect of the sum to be published at least twice at intervals of 14 days in a newspaper
-- circulating in Perth." Regulations reg 10A: "The prescribed amount referred to in section 45(2) of the Act is $1 500."
`the s 45(2) prescribed amount` MEANS 1500

@export Must the Public Trustee advertise an unclaimed estate balance, twice 14 days apart in a Perth newspaper, before it goes to the State (s 45(2), reg 10A)?
GIVEN `the sum` IS A NUMBER @desc The unclaimed sum to the credit of the estate, in dollars
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the Public Trustee advertise the unclaimed sum` IF
    `the sum` AT LEAST `the s 45(2) prescribed amount`

@export May the estate money held for me be paid to the State's Consolidated Account as unclaimed (s 45(1))?
GIVEN `years the sum has been held`                          IS A NUMBER  @desc Years the sum has been held to the credit of the estate before 1 March
      `the Public Trustee knows of someone entitled or claiming` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Public Trustee has any information of a person entitled in distribution, or claiming to be
      `the sum`                                              IS A NUMBER  @desc The sum, in dollars
      `it has been advertised twice 14 days apart`           IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it has been advertised as s 45(2) requires
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the sum go to the Consolidated Account` IF
        `years the sum has been held` AT LEAST 6
    AND (NOT `the Public Trustee knows of someone entitled or claiming`)
    AND (    (NOT `Must the Public Trustee advertise the unclaimed sum` `the sum`)
          OR `it has been advertised twice 14 days apart`)
